❗ 1. compareTo returns 0
👉 Treated as duplicate in TreeSet

❗ 2. No Comparable/Comparator
👉 ❌ ClassCastException

“Comparable ->natural sorting ->inside class->using compareTo(),
- Comparator ->custom sorting-> outside the class ->using compare().
 Comparator is more flexible and allows multiple sorting strategies.”


 _______________________________________________________

 ✅ COMPARABLE (Point-wise)
 -Used for natural/default sorting
 -Defined inside the class
 -Belongs to java.lang package
- Class must implement Comparable interface
 -Uses method:
  compareTo();
 Syntax:
 class A implements Comparable<A>
 -Only one sorting logic possible ❌
 -Modifies the original class
- Automatically used by:
 TreeSet
 TreeMap
 Collections.sort()

 -Sorting is based on:

 obj1.compareTo(obj2)

 Return values:
 Negative → smaller
 Zero → equal
 Positive → greater

 -If compareTo() == 0 → treated as duplicate
 -If not implemented → ❌ ClassCastException
 Less flexible
 -Good for default behavior
 Used when:
 Sorting rule is fixed

 Risk:
 return a - b; // overflow risk

 Better:
 Integer.compare(a, b);

 ✅ COMPARATOR (Point-wise)
 -Used for custom sorting
 -Defined outside the class
 -Belongs to java.util package

 -Uses method:
 compare()
 Syntax:
 Comparator<A>

 -Allows multiple sorting logics ✅
 -Does NOT modify original class
 -Passed as argument to:
 TreeSet
 TreeMap
 Collections.sort()

 -Sorting is based on:
 compare(obj1, obj2)

 -Return values:
 Negative → smaller
 Zero → equal
 Positive → greater
 If compare() == 0 → treated as duplicate in TreeSet
 More flexible
 Used when:
 Multiple sorting needed
 Cannot modify class

 Supports:
 Comparator.comparing()
 Comparator.reverseOrder()
 -Can use:
 Lambda expressions ✅
 Preferred in real projects
 🔥 FINAL QUICK DIFFERENCE

 👉 Comparable = inside + default/natural  soting + single logic
 👉 Comparator = outside + custom sorting + multiple logic